  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nAn amplified meridional mid-level pattern is in place across the \nCONUS today with a trough over the eastern half of the country, \nridging over the Mountainous West and another deep trough axis \napproaching the west coast. For us, northwest flow continues aloft \nfor now. At the surface, a large surface ridge centered over central \nCanada is building into area. The coolest air within this chilly \npattern will be felt tonight through Saturday. Radiational cooling \ndue to clear skies and nearly calm winds tonight should allow \ntemperatures to fall into the upper 20s by sunrise Friday. \nTemperatures will then struggle to reach 50 degrees tomorrow \nafternoon before tanking into the mid 20s tomorrow night. These next\ntwo nights will be the first hard freeze for a majority of the area,\nwhich is anomalously late compared to average.\n\nThe surface pattern won't change much on Saturday with the ridge \nholding strong just northeast of the area. Aloft, however, the \nwestern trough will evolve into a closed low over the TX panhandle \nby early Sunday with a surface low also forming well south of the \narea. Lift associated with that system within an area of better \nmoisture availability will lead to rain development in TX/OK and \npossibly far southern KS. Confidence remains high that northeast KS \nwill stay dry on the north side of that system. Temperatures \nmoderate slightly through the weekend, but will possibly be held \nback slightly due to cloud cover in central KS.\n\nEarly next week, the center of the large surface ridge will move \neastward over the far eastern US, but it will still have some grip \non the area. The approach of another western trough will induce \ncyclogenesis to our west, increasing the pressure gradient. Stronger\nsouthwest flow should boost temperatures back to near 60 degrees by \nthe middle of the week. That is also when rain chances start to \ncreep back into the forecast, although probabilities remain around 20\nto 30 percent with low confidence at this range.",
